---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import WeatherKitAttribution from '../components/WeatherKitAttribution.astro';
import { breadcrumbList, absoluteUrl } from '../config';

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbList([{ name: 'The science', path: '/science' }], 'en'),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
    name: 'The science behind Bronzla',
    url: absoluteUrl('/science'),
    about: {
      '@type': 'MedicalCondition',
      name: 'Sunburn (erythema)',
    },
    lastReviewed: '2026-08-05',
    medicalAudienceType: 'Patient',
    audience: {
      '@type': 'PeopleAudience',
      audienceType: 'General public',
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  locale="en"
  path="/science"
  title="The science behind Bronzla · UV index, MED and safe exposure times"
  description="How Bronzla calculates safe sun exposure times from the WHO UV index and published minimal erythemal dose data, with the full method, worked examples and citation list."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header class="container page-header">
    <h1>The science behind Bronzla</h1>
    <p>
      Every figure Bronzla shows traces back to a published source. This page sets out the full
      method: the phototype data, the minimal erythemal dose values, the UV index definition that
      connects a weather forecast to a burn time, and every citation behind it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="container prose">
    <Callout label="Not medical advice">
      <p>
        Bronzla estimates sun exposure times from population-average figures. It does not know
        your medical history, your medication, your baseline vitamin D status, or how your skin
        actually behaves. Every number below is an estimate with considerable error, and none of
        it should be used to make a medical decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        Photosensitising medication, a history of skin cancer, and conditions such as lupus or
        xeroderma pigmentosum all invalidate this model entirely. See a dermatologist about any
        concern with your skin. If you are burnt, treat it as a burn.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2>Fitzpatrick phototypes</h2>
    <p>
      The Fitzpatrick scale, devised in 1975 and validated in Fitzpatrick's 1988 paper, classifies
      skin by how it responds to ultraviolet light. It is the scale the dermatological literature
      uses when reporting minimal erythemal dose, which is why Bronzla keys off it rather than a
      richer or more modern classification.
    </p>
    <p>
      The descriptions below are written for a Turkish and wider Mediterranean audience: types III
      and IV are by far the most common in Türkiye, so the app describes them on their own terms
      rather than against northern European reference points.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Fitzpatrick skin phototypes I through VI</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Label</th>
            <th scope="col">Behaviour in the sun</th>
            <th scope="col">Recognisable description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>I</td><td>Very fair</td><td>Always burns, never tans</td><td>Red or blond hair, blue or green eyes, freckles. Uncommon in Türkiye.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>II</td><td>Fair</td><td>Burns easily, tans with difficulty</td><td>Blond or light brown hair, light eyes. Common along the Black Sea and in Thrace.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>III</td><td>Light olive</td><td>Sometimes burns, tans gradually</td><td>Brown hair, hazel or brown eyes. One of the two most common types in Türkiye.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>IV</td><td>Olive</td><td>Rarely burns, tans easily</td><td>Dark brown hair and eyes. Most common on the Mediterranean and Aegean coasts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>V</td><td>Brown</td><td>Very rarely burns, tans very easily</td><td>Near black hair, dark eyes. Common in the southeast.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VI</td><td>Deep brown</td><td>Almost never burns, always darkens</td><td>Black hair and eyes. Reddening is very rare.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Bronzla's skin type quiz derives a phototype from seven questions rather than asking someone
      to self-select from a chart, which is a well-known source of error, particularly for
      phototypes III and IV.
    </p>

    <h2>Minimal erythemal dose</h2>
    <p>
      The minimal erythemal dose (MED) is the erythemally weighted ultraviolet energy that
      produces just-perceptible reddening, assessed 24 hours after exposure, on unadapted skin.
      These are midpoints of the ranges reported in the clinical literature: population averages,
      not personal measurements. An individual's true MED can sit well outside the value for their
      phototype, which is one of the reasons Bronzla applies a safety fraction on top of the raw
      calculation (see below).
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Minimal erythemal dose by phototype</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">MED (erythemally weighted J/m²)</th>
            <th scope="col">Equivalent in SED</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>I</td><td>200</td><td>2.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>II</td><td>250</td><td>2.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>III</td><td>300</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>IV</td><td>450</td><td>4.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>V</td><td>600</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VI</td><td>1000</td><td>10.0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>A warning about units</h3>
    <p>
      The figures above are <strong>erythemally weighted joules per square metre</strong>. The
      standard erythema dose is defined as 1 SED = 100 J/m² of erythemally weighted UV, so the
      table spans 2 to 10 SED. <strong>These figures are not comparable to unweighted physical
      doses quoted in mJ/cm².</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Older solar-simulator studies frequently report MED as a raw radiometric dose in mJ/cm²,
      measured over a lamp's whole output band with no action spectrum applied. Typical numbers
      there run in the tens of mJ/cm², and a naive unit conversion makes the table above look
      wrong by roughly an order of magnitude. It is not wrong; the two quantities measure
      different things.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Erythemally weighted dose versus unweighted solar-simulator dose</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Erythemally weighted dose</th>
            <th scope="col">Unweighted solar-simulator dose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Typical unit</td><td>J/m² (or SED)</td><td>mJ/cm²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spectrum</td><td>CIE erythema action spectrum applied</td><td>None, raw lamp output over its band</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Depends on source spectrum</td><td>No, that is the point of the weighting</td><td>Yes, heavily</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Comparable to a UV index</td><td>Yes, directly</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Bronzla's model is built entirely on the erythemally weighted convention, because the UV
      index is defined in the same terms. Mixing the two would produce an app that tells someone
      with fair skin they have several hours, which is exactly the failure mode this section
      exists to prevent.
    </p>

    <h2>The core calculation</h2>
    <p>
      The UV index is defined, by the World Health Organization and World Meteorological
      Organization, such that one index unit equals 25 mW/m² of erythemally weighted irradiance.
      That single definition is what connects a weather forecast to a burn time.
    </p>
    <pre><code>irradiance (W/m²) = uvIndex &times; 0.025
time to burn (s)  = MED (J/m²) / irradiance &times; protectionFactor</code></pre>
    <p>
      Worked example: phototype III, UV index 8, bare skin.
    </p>
    <pre><code>irradiance = 8 &times; 0.025      = 0.2 W/m&sup2;
burn time  = 300 / 0.2      = 1500 s = 25 minutes</code></pre>
    <p>which agrees with published erythema tables. Burn times at UV 8 on bare skin, for reference:</p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Time to one MED at UV index 8, bare skin</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Time to one MED</th>
            <th scope="col">Bronzla's recommended session</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>I</td><td>17 min</td><td>10 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>II</td><td>21 min</td><td>13 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>III</td><td>25 min</td><td>15 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>IV</td><td>38 min</td><td>23 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>V</td><td>50 min</td><td>30 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VI</td><td>83 min</td><td>50 min</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The safety fraction</h3>
    <p>
      Bronzla's recommended session is 60 per cent of the calculated burn time, not the burn time
      itself. Staying below one MED is what separates tanning from burning: melanogenesis is
      stimulated well before reddening appears, so a sub-erythemal session still produces a tan.
      The remaining 40 per cent is headroom for the model's own error, which is considerable:
      population-average MED, forecast UV rather than measured UV, no account of albedo from sand
      or water, no account of altitude, and no account of how much of the body is actually in the
      sun.
    </p>

    <h3>The three-hour cap</h3>
    <p>
      Bronzla never recommends more than three hours in a single session, whatever the arithmetic
      says. <strong>This is a safety policy, not a clinical figure.</strong> There is no paper
      behind three hours. Two things motivate it. First, the linear-dose assumption underlying the
      whole model degrades over long sessions: forecast UV drifts from actual UV, sunscreen wears
      off, people move in and out of shade, and cumulative photodamage is not usefully modelled as
      a single dose integral. Second, no app should recommend six hours in the sun, regardless of
      phototype. The cap binds most often for phototypes V and VI in moderate UV, and for anyone
      wearing high SPF, and in those cases Bronzla is deliberately more conservative than its own
      maths.
    </p>

    <h2>Sunscreen</h2>
    <p>
      SPF is measured under ISO 24444 at an application rate of 2 mg per square centimetre of
      skin. Nobody applies that much. Petersen and Wulf (2014) found real-world application is
      roughly a third of the test dose, and protection falls off faster than linearly with
      thickness, so effective protection sits well below the label figure. Bronzla applies a flat
      50 per cent efficiency discount to every SPF value rather than using the label figure
      directly.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Nominal SPF against the protection factor Bronzla uses</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Nominal SPF</th><th scope="col">Protection factor used</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 (bare skin)</td><td>1.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>8.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td>15.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td>25.5</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is a modelling choice rather than a measurement, and a deliberately conservative one.
      The alternative is telling someone wearing SPF 50 that they have twenty hours, which is both
      false and dangerous. Bronzla fixes reapplication at every two hours, sooner after swimming
      or towelling, in line with every product label and dermatology body, and recommends SPF 30
      or higher for every phototype, following the American Academy of Dermatology's guidance for
      broad-spectrum protection regardless of skin type.
    </p>

    <h2>Vitamin D</h2>
    <p>
      Bronzla's vitamin D estimate has three parts, with very different levels of evidence behind
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The plateau is real.</strong> Synthesis self-limits at approximately one MED, as
      previtamin D photoisomerises to inert products, so further exposure yields no more vitamin D
      while continuing to accumulate erythemal dose. This is the single most useful thing the
      estimate communicates: more sun does not mean more vitamin D.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The whole-body yield is drawn from the literature.</strong> Holick (2011) reports
      that whole-body exposure to one MED is comparable to an oral intake of roughly 10,000 to
      25,000 IU. Bronzla uses 15,000 IU, near the lower middle of that range.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The melanin multipliers have no published basis.</strong> Melanin competes with
      7-dehydrocholesterol for the same photons, so darker phototypes do need materially longer
      exposure for the same yield; that direction is well described in the literature. The
      specific multiplier Bronzla applies for each phototype is not: it encodes a plausible shape,
      not a validated figure, and the app labels the vitamin D estimate as an estimate wherever it
      appears. It should never be used to decide on supplementation.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      Apple's App Review guideline 1.4.1 requires medical citations to be easy for the user to
      find, not merely present somewhere. Bronzla surfaces this exact list from every screen that
      gives exposure guidance.
    </p>

    <h3>UV index</h3>
    <ol class="citations">
      <li>
        <cite>World Health Organization.</cite> <em>Radiation: The Ultraviolet (UV) Index.</em>
        WHO Questions and Answers.
        <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/radiation-the-ultraviolet-(uv)-index" rel="noopener">who.int</a>.
        Backs: one UV index unit equals 25 mW/m² of erythemally weighted irradiance, and the
        protection advice for each band.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>International Commission on Non-Ionizing Radiation Protection.</cite>
        <em>UV Index.</em> ICNIRP.
        <a href="https://www.icnirp.org/en/applications/uv-index/index.html" rel="noopener">icnirp.org</a>.
        Backs: the Low, Moderate, High, Very High and Extreme band thresholds.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Skin type</h3>
    <ol class="citations">
      <li>
        <cite>Fitzpatrick TB (1988).</cite> <em>The validity and practicality of sun-reactive skin
        types I through VI.</em> Archives of Dermatology, 124(6), 869-871.
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3377516/" rel="noopener">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>.
        Backs: the six phototypes and the burning and tanning behaviour described for each.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Sunscreen</h3>
    <ol class="citations">
      <li>
        <cite>International Organization for Standardization (2019).</cite> <em>ISO 24444:2019
        Cosmetics. Sun protection test methods. In vivo determination of the sun protection factor
        (SPF).</em> <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/72250.html" rel="noopener">iso.org</a>.
        Backs: SPF is measured at an application rate of 2 mg per square centimetre of skin.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>Petersen B, Wulf HC (2014).</cite> <em>Application of sunscreen: theory and
        reality.</em> Photodermatology, Photoimmunology and Photomedicine.
        <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/phpp.12099" rel="noopener">onlinelibrary.wiley.com</a>.
        Backs: people apply far less than the test dose in practice, so real-world protection sits
        well below the label figure.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>American Academy of Dermatology.</cite> <em>How to apply sunscreen.</em>
        <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener">aad.org</a>.
        Backs: reapply every two hours, and immediately after swimming or sweating.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>American Academy of Dermatology.</cite> <em>Practice Safe Sun.</em>
        <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/practice-safe-sun" rel="noopener">aad.org</a>.
        Backs: broad spectrum SPF 30 or higher is recommended for everyone.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Vitamin D</h3>
    <ol class="citations">
      <li>
        <cite>Holick MF (2011).</cite> <em>Vitamin D: a D-lightful solution for health.</em>
        Journal of Internal Medicine.
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21415774/" rel="noopener">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>.
        Backs: whole-body exposure to one minimal erythemal dose is comparable to an oral intake
        of roughly 10,000 to 25,000 IU.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Aftercare</h3>
    <ol class="citations">
      <li>
        <cite>American Academy of Dermatology.</cite> <em>How to treat a sunburn.</em>
        <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/injured-skin/burns/treat-sunburn" rel="noopener">aad.org</a>.
        Backs: cool baths, aloe vera or soy products, and never popping blisters.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>Mayo Clinic.</cite> <em>Sunburn: First aid.</em>
        <a href="https://www.mayoclinic.org/first-aid/first-aid-sunburn/basics/art-20056643" rel="noopener">mayoclinic.org</a>.
        Backs: cool compresses, and that applying ice directly damages sunburnt skin.
      </li>
      <li>
        <cite>Cleveland Clinic.</cite> <em>When to get care for a sunburn.</em>
        <a href="https://health.clevelandclinic.org/when-to-get-care-for-a-sunburn" rel="noopener">health.clevelandclinic.org</a>.
        Backs: when to seek medical care, namely dehydration, chills, nausea or extensive
        blistering.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <WeatherKitAttribution locale="en" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem) clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
  }

  .page-header p {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .prose {
    padding-block: 1rem clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
</style>
